---
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
---

<Docs
	page="embedded"
	title="Embedded SQL"
	lede="How squill finds SQL inside host code, what it will and won't rewrite, and how to point it somewhere new."
>
	<section id="built-ins">
		<h2>What gets formatted</h2>
		<p>
			An <a href="docs/configuration/#rules"><code>[[embedded]]</code> rule</a>{" "}
			tells squill which files hold SQL: it parses them with a tree-sitter
			grammar, runs an extraction query over the tree to find the strings
			holding SQL, and reformats those in place. The rest of the file is
			untouched &mdash; squill never reprints host code, only the string
			contents it was pointed at.
		</p>
		<Code lang="toml" file="squill.toml" code={`[[embedded]]
include = ["**/*.rs"]
grammar = "rust"`} />
		<p>Built-in grammars, and what their default queries find:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<img style="height: 16px; vertical-align: -2px;" src="langs/rust.svg" alt="" />
				<code>rust</code>: sqlx&rsquo;s <code class="boxed">query!</code>,{" "}
				<code class="boxed">query_as!</code>, <code class="boxed"
					>query_scalar!</code
				>, <code class="boxed">query_unchecked!</code> macros and{" "}
				<code class="boxed">query</code>/<code class="boxed">query_as</code>/<code
					class="boxed">query_scalar</code
				> functions
			</li>
			<li>
				<img style="height: 16px; vertical-align: -2px;" src="langs/go.svg" alt="" />
				<code>go</code>: <code class="boxed">Query</code>,{" "}
				<code class="boxed">QueryRow</code>, <code class="boxed">Exec</code> and
				their <code class="boxed">Context</code> variants
			</li>
			<li>
				<img style="height: 16px; vertical-align: -2px;" src="langs/python.svg" alt="" />
				<code>python</code>: <code class="boxed">execute</code>-family methods
				and SQLAlchemy&rsquo;s <code class="boxed">text(...)</code>, with{" "}
				<code>%s</code> / <code>%(name)s</code> params preserved
				(<code>pyformat-params</code> is on for this grammar unless
				configured)
			</li>
			<li>
				<img style="height: 16px; vertical-align: -2px;" src="langs/typescript.svg" alt="" />
				<code>javascript</code>, <code>typescript</code>, <code>tsx</code>:{" "}
				<code class="boxed">.query</code>/<code class="boxed">.execute</code>/<code
					class="boxed">.prepare</code
				> calls and <code class="boxed">sql`&hellip;`</code> tagged templates
			</li>
			<li>
				<img style="height: 16px; vertical-align: -2px;" src="langs/gleam.svg" alt="" />
				<code>gleam</code>: <code class="boxed">sqlight</code> (as SQLite) and{" "}
				<code class="boxed">pog</code> (as the configured dialect)
			</li>
			<li>
				<code>cpp</code>: raw strings passed to <code class="boxed"
					>sqlite3_prepare*</code
				>/<code class="boxed">sqlite3_exec</code> (as SQLite), libpq&rsquo;s{" "}
				<code class="boxed">PQexec</code>-family (as Postgres), and libpqxx-style{" "}
				<code class="boxed">exec</code>/<code class="boxed">query</code> methods
			</li>
			<li>
				<code>csharp</code>: raw strings passed to EF Core&rsquo;s{" "}
				<code class="boxed">migrationBuilder.Sql</code> and raw-SQL methods,
				Dapper&rsquo;s query/execute family, or assigned to{" "}
				<code class="boxed">CommandText</code>, with <code>@name</code>{" "}
				params preserved (<code>at-params</code> is on for this grammar
				unless configured)
			</li>
			<li>
				<code>java</code>, <code>kotlin</code>: text blocks and raw strings
				passed to JDBC, JPA, Spring <code class="boxed">JdbcTemplate</code>, or
				Exposed calls, with JDBC&rsquo;s <code>?</code> placeholders preserved
				(<code>question-params</code> is on for these grammars unless
				configured)
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The <a href="docs/recipes/rust/">recipes</a> show each of these at
			work, library by library, along with queries for the libraries the
			defaults don&rsquo;t cover.
		</p>
		<p>
			A file named explicitly on the command line that no rule covers formats
			as plain SQL &mdash; unless it is in a language squill has a grammar for,
			in which case squill stops and shows the rule to add.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="strings">
		<h2>Which strings are eligible</h2>
		<p>
			Only <em>multiline string syntaxes</em> are rewritten: Rust raw strings,
			Go backticks, Python triple quotes, JS template literals, Gleam strings,
			C++ and C# raw strings, Java text blocks, and Kotlin raw strings. Those
			already support a vertical layout, so squill puts the quotes on their own
			lines and formats one clause per line.
		</p>
		<p>
			A plain Rust or Go string joins them once it already spans lines &mdash;
			written, escaped (<code>\n</code>), or continued &mdash; and is rewritten
			as a raw string (<code>r#"&hellip;"#</code>, or backticks in Go), so the
			SQL reads as written with no escaped quotes. A single-line plain string
			stays byte-identical.
		</p>
		<div class="note">
			<p>
				Strings with interpolation holes are never touched &mdash; Python
				f-strings, <code>$&lbrace;&hellip;&rbrace;</code> in JS templates,
				C#&rsquo;s <code>$"""</code>, and Kotlin&rsquo;s <code>$name</code>{" "}
				templates. SQL with a hole in it is not SQL squill can parse, so it
				leaves it exactly as written.
			</p>
		</div>
		<h3>Safety</h3>
		<p>
			Every rewritten string passes the same safety check as a{" "}
			<code>.sql</code> file, and then the host file is parsed again: an edit
			that would add a syntax error, or whose string no longer reads back
			through the query exactly as written, is dropped. A string squill
			declines to rewrite &mdash; SQL that doesn&rsquo;t parse, an escape it
			can&rsquo;t decode, a result the string can&rsquo;t hold &mdash; is
			reported as a diagnostic with its line and column, and fails the run
			under <code>--strict</code>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="queries">
		<h2>Writing your own query</h2>
		<p>
			A rule&rsquo;s <code>query</code> replaces its grammar&rsquo;s default
			with a tree-sitter query file, found relative to the config. A capture
			named <code>@sql</code> marks a string whose contents are SQL in the
			configured dialect; <code>@sql.postgres</code> and{" "}
			<code>@sql.sqlite</code> pin the dialect regardless of the{" "}
			<code>dialect</code> setting.
		</p>
		<Code lang="toml" file="squill.toml" code={`[[embedded]]
include = ["**/*.rs"]
grammar = "rust"
query = ".config/squill/rust.scm"`} />
		<Code lang="query" file=".config/squill/rust.scm" code={`((macro_invocation
   macro: (identifier) @_name
   (token_tree (raw_string_literal) @sql))
 (#eq? @_name "my_sql"))`} />
		<p>
			Captures whose names start with <code>_</code> are the usual tree-sitter
			convention for "match this, but it isn't the thing I want" &mdash; squill
			only rewrites <code>@sql</code> captures.{" "}
			<a href="docs/cli/#locate"><code>squill locate</code></a> lists what a
			query captures, file by file, so you can check it before formatting
			anything.
		</p>
		<h3>Supported predicates</h3>
		<p>
			<code>#eq?</code>, <code>#not-eq?</code>, and <code>#any-of?</code>.
			Regex predicates (<code>#match?</code>, <code>#not-match?</code>) are
			rejected up front, and so is any other custom predicate: a predicate the
			engine does not evaluate would silently widen your query to match things
			you never intended, so squill refuses the query instead of quietly
			over-matching.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="grammars">
		<h2>Bringing your own grammar</h2>
		<p>
			Any language with a tree-sitter grammar works: build it to wasm with{" "}
			<code>tree-sitter build --wasm</code> (many grammars publish one with
			each release) and point a rule at the file. The language name comes from
			the file name, the way that command writes it:{" "}
			<code>tree-sitter-lua.wasm</code> holds <code>lua</code>. A loaded
			grammar has no default query, so the rule needs one.
		</p>
		<Code lang="toml" file="squill.toml" code={`[[embedded]]
include = ["**/*.lua"]
grammar = ".config/squill/tree-sitter-lua.wasm"
query = ".config/squill/lua.scm"
dialect = "sqlite"`} />
		<Code lang="query" file=".config/squill/lua.scm" code={`((function_call
   name: (method_index_expression method: (identifier) @_m)
   arguments: (arguments . (string) @sql))
 (#eq? @_m "exec"))`} />
		<p>
			squill knows nothing about a loaded grammar&rsquo;s string syntax, so it
			takes the conservative road. Capture either the string&rsquo;s content
			node or the whole literal (squill takes the text between its opening
			and closing delimiter tokens); it is rewritten only when it already
			spans lines &mdash; proof the syntax takes raw newlines &mdash; and
			never when it holds a backslash that might be an escape. The re-parse
			check catches the rest.
		</p>
		<h3 id="urls">From a URL</h3>
		<p>
			A grammar can also come straight from where it&rsquo;s published, as an{" "}
			<code>https</code> URL naming the <code>.wasm</code> file:
		</p>
		<Code lang="toml" file="squill.toml" code={`[[embedded]]
include = ["**/*.lua"]
grammar = "https://github.com/tree-sitter-grammars/tree-sitter-lua/releases/download/v0.5.0/tree-sitter-lua.wasm"
query = ".config/squill/lua.scm"`} />
		<p>
			The first time squill downloads it, it records the grammar&rsquo;s
			SHA-256 in a <code>squill.lock</code> beside the config; commit that
			file. Every later download must match it, or squill stops and shows
			both hashes. Downloads are cached by hash in your user cache directory
			(<code>$XDG_CACHE_HOME/squill</code>, or the platform&rsquo;s own), so
			a locked grammar that&rsquo;s already cached never touches the network.
			In CI, <code>squill fmt --locked</code> refuses to record anything new:
			a grammar URL missing from <code>squill.lock</code> is an error instead
			of a download trusted on sight.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="indentation">
		<h2>Indentation</h2>
		<p>
			Embedded SQL is anchored to the host statement&rsquo;s indentation and,
			by default, copies that file&rsquo;s own indent character, so a
			spaces-indented file never gains tabs. Set <code>indent</code> in the{" "}
			<a href="docs/configuration/#rules">rule</a> (or at the top level) to
			choose deliberately instead. Lines inside a SQL string that itself spans
			lines are data, and are never re-indented.
		</p>
	</section>
</Docs>
